\description{
Robust Aitchison PCA (\code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}) of a count table expanded to every node of
a phylogenetic tree: each node's count is the total of the tips below it,
weighted by its branch length. Clades that change together then act as
one feature, and the result is a compositional counterpart of a UniFrac
PCoA, with loadings for every clade. This is gemelli's
\code{phylogenetic_rpca()} (Martino et al. 2022), QIIME 2's
\verb{gemelli phylogenetic-rpca}.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Filtering.} The tips (features) and samples are filtered exactly as
by \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, except that samples with a single non-zero tip are kept:
they have counts on that tip's ancestors too.
\item \strong{The tree.} The tree is cut down to its root, the tips with counts
after filtering, and their ancestors. A node left with a single child
is merged into it, adding its branch length to the child's. The root
has no length (gemelli drops it), unless it is left with a single
child: then the two become one node, the root, whose length is the
child's (after the merges above) plus the root length,
\code{tree$root.edge} (trees from \code{\link[=read_tree]{read_tree()}} have none). Then, with
\code{min_depth} above 0, each internal node with at most \code{min_depth} tips
below it is removed, and its tips are attached, in order, after its
parent's other children.
\item \strong{Node table.} Each node's count in a sample is the total count of
the tips below it (a tip's is its own count). Internal nodes are
numbered as scikit-bio's \code{TreeNode.assign_ids()} numbers them: visiting
the nodes in postorder, each node's children are numbered in turn, and
the root last; they are named \code{n} followed by that number (from 0).
The columns of the node table, and the rows of \code{loadings}, are in
this order.
\item \strong{Robust clr with branch lengths.} For each sample, the log of each
node's count times its branch length, minus their mean over the
sample's observed entries. An entry is observed when both the count
and the branch length are positive. So a node with length 0 (such as
the root, usually) is a feature with no
observed values, which still counts in OptSpace's scaling and in the
final centering, and has loadings. A sample whose observed values are
all equal (for example one with a single tip, whose path to the root
has branches of equal length) has a robust clr of 0 throughout, and
has no observed values: as in gemelli, its position only reflects
the other samples.
\item \strong{Matrix completion and PCA}, exactly as \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}.
}

\code{rpca_phylo()} can also be run as \code{count_ord(counts, 'rpca_phylo', tree = tree)}.

\strong{Speed and memory}

The node table is never formed densely: each sample's node counts are
summed from its non-zero tips, in parallel, and the rest runs as for
\code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, whose "Speed and memory" notes apply with the nodes as
features. The tree is processed in time proportional to its size. Results
are identical from run to run and for any number of \code{cpus}.

\strong{Differences from gemelli}

\code{rpca_phylo()} is a port of \code{gemelli.rpca.phylogenetic_rpca()} (gemelli
0.0.13), with the tree handling of iow 1.0.8 and scikit-bio 0.7.4 that it
calls. On the package's test tables (including gemelli's test tree),
the node counts are the same and the results agree with gemelli's to
within \eqn{2 \times 10^{-14}} (relative). The differences are those of \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}
(its "Differences from gemelli" section: \code{max_iters} is used where gemelli
ignores \code{max_iterations} and always runs 5 iterations, \code{n_axes} may be 1,
axes of rounding-error variance are dropped, \code{eig} and the scores are
scaled as \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}'s), and these:
\itemize{
\item gemelli converts counts to whole numbers (64-bit integers) before
summing them up the tree, so proportions become zeros. \code{rpca_phylo()}
uses the counts as given.
\item Samples with a single non-zero tip are kept, as gemelli keeps them
(unlike \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, which drops samples with a single non-zero feature).
\item gemelli's OptSpace treats any robust clr value that is exactly 0 as
missing, which depends on rounding. Here an entry is missing exactly
when its count or its branch length is 0, or when all of its sample's
values are equal.
\item The root length is \code{tree$root.edge}. ape's \code{read.tree()} keeps a
Newick root length there, but ecodive's \code{\link[=read_tree]{read_tree()}} discards it. It
only matters when the root is left with a single child: then, for a
tree read by \code{\link[=read_tree]{read_tree()}}, the merged root's length lacks the root
length that gemelli, reading the same Newick file, would add.
\item Internal node labels in \code{tree} are replaced by the \verb{n<id>} names, as
gemelli does; gemelli's optional taxonomy output is not provided.
}
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last, the records
of how it was made (see \link{ecodive_steps}): \code{dist}, the distance
matrix's, for the methods that take distances, and \code{ord}, the
ordination's own (its method, display name and settings). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

